    factG1: 'SPHEREx launched on 11 March 2025 from Vandenberg, California, on a Falcon 9, sharing the ride with NASA’s PUNCH mission.',
    factG2: 'It maps the whole sky in 102 colours of infrared light, from 0.75 to 5 µm.',
    factG3: 'One full map of the sky takes about six months. The two-year mission plans four of them.',
    factG4: 'Its telescope is only 20 cm across. The power is in the colours: a spectrum for every point of the sky.',
    factG5: 'Three cone-shaped shields keep it cold without any refrigerant: the telescope works at about −210 °C.',
    factG6: 'Each detector sits behind a filter whose colour changes from one edge to the other. As the sky drifts across, a star is measured in one colour after another.',
    factG7: 'It has six detectors of 2048 × 2048 pixels; each pixel sees 6.15″ of sky.',
    factG8: 'It measures hundreds of millions of galaxies to test what happened in the first fraction of a second after the Big Bang (inflation).',
    factG9: 'It looks for frozen water, carbon dioxide and carbon monoxide on dust grains in our Galaxy: the raw material of planets.',
    factG10: 'It adds up the total glow of all galaxies, including the faint ones no telescope sees one by one.',
    factG11: 'Near the north and south ecliptic poles its scans overlap, so those two regions are observed far more often and go much deeper.',
    factG12: 'Infrared light passes through dust that hides stars from visible-light telescopes.',
    factG13: 'Water ice absorbs near 3 µm and CO₂ ice near 4.27 µm: SPHEREx sees these dips in the spectra of stars behind cold clouds.',
    factG14: 'All SPHEREx images are public at NASA/IPAC IRSA. Radians reads them straight from there.',
    factT1: 'Four times as many exposures ≈ half the noise: noise falls as 1/√N.',
    factT2: 'Noise is different in every exposure, stars are the same. That is why stacking keeps the stars and averages the noise away.',
    factT3: 'If the star count stops rising while the noise keeps falling, the limit is crowding: at 6″ per pixel, stars start to blend.',
    factT4: 'Edges get fewer exposures (see Coverage), so they stay noisier than the middle.',
    factT5: 'A moving star smears out or splits in two when exposures from different dates are stacked. Blink shows it better.',
